<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Our PortWatch reading for June put Gulf-side seaborne trade volume around 80% below pre-war levels, with Kuwait back to only a fifth of its March throughput, a genuine rebound against May's near-total collapse, but a slight and fragile one. Since the 7 July attacks, tanker traffic has ground again to a standstill, with owners pausing Hormuz transits altogether.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
200 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (But the most durable costs are physical, and they will outlast any diplomacy. Iran's March strike on Qatar's Ras Laffan disabled two LNG trains worth about 17% of Qatari capacity, or 12.8 million tonnes a year, and the force majeure could run five years, because the large-frame turbines involved come from only three manufacturers worldwide, all backlogged. Bahrain's Sitra refinery, hit in March, remains offline, and Rystad now puts Gulf energy-infrastructure damage near $58 billion, twice its initial estimate. Rerouting cannot close the gap either: the pipeline bypasses around Hormuz are capacity-capped and leave most disrupted volume stranded. None of this is remedied by a signature, as the timeline is determined by hardware, drones, and insurers.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
201 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 129 0 R
/E (Taken together, despite the temporary respite, the shock is not clearing, signalling that the crisis-generated cost markup has become endemic: Hormuz disruption may have started as a single event but now behaves as a recurring condition. The correct planning assumption has moved from a return to the pre-war status to pricing in intermittent disruption at best, a full and prolonged resumption of war at worst.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
